title: "stdlib-set-tutorial"
author: money-agent (AI-assisted, human-reviewed before submission)
date: 2026-11-28
abstract: A hands-on tour of stdlib/set.ltl: sets as sorted, deduplicated lists, the full algebra (union, intersection, difference, symmetric difference, power set), and the two order-related behaviours the source pins down, with a runnable tag-overlap example and pinned test vectors checked line by line against the module source.

# stdlib.set: Sets Are Just Sorted Lists

Plenty of everyday questions are set questions in disguise: which
tags do these two articles share, which permissions does this role
have that the other lacks, which feature flags are on for exactly
one group? `stdlib/set.ltl` answers them with a deliberately simple
representation — a set is a **sorted list with no duplicates** — so
every result is deterministic, printable, and comparable with `==`.

Public functions from the module are called with the `set_` prefix,
the same convention the spiral test suites use for every stdlib
module (compare `aho_corasick_build` for `stdlib/aho_corasick.ltl`).

## Building Sets

`set_new()` is the empty list. `set_from_list` deduplicates and
sorts in one pass, and `set_add` returns a new set with the element
inserted in sorted position (adding an existing element returns the
set unchanged):

    set_from_list([3, 1, 2, 1, 3])        // [1, 2, 3]
    set_from_list(["banana", "apple", "banana"])
                                          // ["apple", "banana"]
    set_add([1, 2, 3], 4)                 // [1, 2, 3, 4]
    set_add([1, 2, 3], 2)                 // [1, 2, 3]

`set_remove` filters the element out, `set_has` tests membership,
`set_size` is the length, and `set_to_list` re-sorts any list into
canonical set order.

## The Algebra

The four workhorses, on `[1, 2, 3, 4]` and `[2, 4, 6]`:

    set_union([1, 2, 3], [3, 4])          // [1, 2, 3, 4]
    set_intersection([1, 2, 3, 4], [2, 4, 6])
                                          // [2, 4]
    set_difference([1, 2, 3, 4], [2, 4])  // [1, 3]
    set_symmetric_difference([1, 2, 3], [3, 4])
                                          // [1, 2, 4]

Predicates cover the comparisons: `set_is_subset`,
`set_is_superset`, `set_is_disjoint`, and `set_equals`. Equality is
defined as "same length and one is a subset of the other", which
makes it **order-insensitive** — a genuinely useful property when
one side was built by hand:

    set_equals([1, 2], [2, 1])            // true

`set_map_set` maps a function over a set and re-canonicalises, so
collisions collapse: mapping `[1, 2, 3, 4]` through `x % 2` gives
the set `[0, 1]`. `set_filter_set` keeps the elements a predicate
accepts, `set_fold` reduces in sorted order, and `set_power_set`
enumerates every subset, smallest structure first:

    set_power_set([1, 2])
    // [[], [2], [1], [1, 2]]

## One Order Quirk, Pinned Rather Than Hidden

`set_union` and `set_symmetric_difference` sort their results, but
`set_intersection` (and `set_difference`) simply filter the left
operand — they keep **its** order instead of sorting:

    set_intersection([3, 1, 2], [1, 2, 3])   // [3, 1, 2]

Feed both functions proper sets (already sorted, as `set_from_list`
returns) and the distinction never shows. Feed a hand-built unsorted
list and the result follows the left operand. The test vectors pin
this behaviour exactly as the source implements it.

## Runnable Example: Who Shares Which Tags?

The companion file `examples/set_tutorial.ltl` builds two readers'
tag sets and reports the overlap:

    alice tags: 4
    bob tags: 4
    shared tags: 2
      lateralus
      stdlib
    alice-only tags: 2
      bounty
      tutorial
    combined tags: 6

Note the duplicate `"tutorial"` in Alice's raw list: `set_from_list`
removes it before any algebra happens, which is why her set has 4
elements, not 5.

## Pinned Test Vectors

The companion file `tests/set_tutorial_vectors.ltl` pins 35 checks
in the spiral-suite style: construction and deduplication, the full
algebra table, the predicates, order-insensitive equality, the
intersection order quirk, map/filter/fold, and the power-set
enumeration for sets of size 0 to 3. Every expected list was
recomputed from the module source line by line. Run it the same way
as any other suite in `tests/`.

## When to Reach for It

Use `stdlib.set` for the small, exact collections scripts are full
of: tag and permission sets, flag comparisons, allow/deny lists,
deduplicating pipeline stages. Membership is a linear scan, so for
very large collections reach for a hash-based structure instead —
but for the sizes where you can print the whole set and eyeball it,
sorted-list sets are hard to beat: the value you see is the value
you compare.

